prompt:
Use case: precise-object-edit / photorealistic-natural
Edit image 1 into the final wide campaign portrait. Image 1 is the portrait/composition edit target. Image 2 is the user's definitive NEW earbud design reference and must completely replace the old earbuds in image 1. Preserve the transparent background. Wide 2.4:1 landscape; use very high native resolution with exceptional facial detail, ideally 3840 x 1600 if available.

Woman: keep the same relaxed adult woman, ordinary black crew-neck shirt, hair tied back, centered head-and-shoulders composition. She faces STRAIGHT AHEAD with chin level, both eyes gently CLOSED, lips naturally closed. Refine her appearance modestly so she is slightly more conventionally attractive: subtle harmonious cheekbone and jaw contours, tidy natural brows, softly flattering features and minimal natural makeup. Still a believable everyday woman, not a glamour model. Preserve natural asymmetry and an authentic human expression. No head tilt, no lifted chin, no ecstatic pose, no glossy artificial skin or exaggerated lips.

Skin detail is critical: highly resolved authentic pores, tiny freckles, subtle natural color variation, fine vellus hair, delicate real lines around eyelids and lips, individual eyebrow hairs and eyelashes. Crisp fine detail in focus without crunchy sharpening. Skin looks healthy and naturally luminous from soft diffuse daylight, not airbrushed, waxy, oily, plasticky or synthetically noise-textured. Beautiful detailed photographic skin. Neutral realistic skin color and a soft photographic highlight, not flat front flash.

Earbuds: exactly two small LIFE-SIZE devices, one floating beside each ear, with inward-facing silicone tips and visible gaps. Use Image 2's earbud design precisely:
- matte finely textured BLACK faceplate with a narrow muted champagne perimeter, NOT a sunburst brushed-metal disc;
- compact smoke-translucent dark housing with subtle visible internal construction;
- plain curved black soft wing, with NO gold contact on its upper outer surface;
- two small champagne oval contacts stacked on the INNER housing next to a small green-tinted rectangular sensor window;
- darker smoky-gray translucent silicone ear tip with small champagne inlays, NOT pale silicone with large black slots;
- slim black braided cables hang naturally downward outside her shoulders.
Use the reference for shape/materials only, not scale or background. Do not include the inline controller or USB plug. Keep each complete earbud no taller than the woman's own visible outer ear. They should be normal sized accessories, NOT large product closeups. Move them a little farther from the ears to leave room for the larger sound waves, approximately half the width of an outer ear to one outer-ear width of air. Keep woman and both earbuds comfortably inside the central 55% of the wide canvas for mobile framing.

Sound waves: replace the small gray arcs with larger, more visible flowing COLOURFUL sound waves. Three or four thin elegant curved strands on each side flow from the silicone tips toward the corresponding ear. Smooth curves with gentle wave-like rising and falling shapes, about twice the visual extent of the original. A tasteful gradient moving through cyan/blue, violet and soft magenta makes the lines clearly colorful against black. Slight translucency, crisp elegant continuous lines, very restrained luminosity. No beam cones, starbursts, bright points at the ears, scattered particles, rainbow fog, thick neon tubes or loops encircling her head. Do not cover her face or skin with the effects.

Background: fully transparent alpha in all empty space around the woman, between the wave lines, and around the earbuds. NO lighter background behind her head, NO gray halo, NO colored background, NO scenery, NO solid rectangle. No backdrop color from Image 2. Woman and devices fully opaque, fine hair edges clean, waves selectively translucent. Keep the same wide composition with generous transparent outer margins. Shirt softly fades into transparency at the bottom. No text, logos or watermark.
